<TopicPage slug="subjects/math/foundational/equations-and-inequalities">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    An equation is a balanced scale: <strong>whatever you do to one side, do to the other</strong>,
    until the unknown stands alone. Inequalities ask the same question with "which side is
    bigger" — and obey the same rules with one crucial twist. Solve enough of these by hand and
    the moves become reflexes; algebra is a physical skill as much as an idea.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the one rule that surprises everyone — dividing by a negative flips the inequality" widget="equationStep" config={ineqCfg} />

  <h2>The toolkit, in one list</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Undo in reverse order:</strong> strip away addition/subtraction first, then multiplication/division, then powers — peeling the expression like an onion.</li>
    <li><strong>Do it to both sides</strong> — except dividing by the unknown itself, which can throw away solutions (x² = x has two answers; dividing by x keeps only one).</li>
    <li><strong>Check by substituting back.</strong> Ten seconds of checking catches most algebra slips ever made.</li>
    <li><strong>Inequality direction flips</strong> only when multiplying/dividing by a negative — or when taking reciprocals of positive numbers.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Everything later in this ladder — quadratics, calculus, analysis — is these moves composed
    with new objects. Get fluent here and the tower stays stable.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, Elementary Algebra; standard school algebra curricula.</p>
</TopicPage>
